\title{A Digit-Multiset Framework for Vampire Numbers\\ and Their Modular Signatures}
\author{Aristotle}
\date{2026-07-04}

\begin{document}

\begin{abstract}
A \emph{vampire number} is a positive integer $v$ with an even number of decimal digits that factors as $v = x \cdot y$, where the two \emph{fangs} $x$ and $y$ each have half as many digits as $v$ and together use exactly the digits of $v$, rearranged. We develop the theory of vampire numbers---and a wider \emph{bestiary} of related numerical monsters (werewolves, ghosts, and zombies)---from a single organizing principle: the invariance of the \textbf{multiset of decimal digits} across a fang factorization. We prove, using only multiset arithmetic, that both the digit sum and the digit count are additive across a fang pair. From digit-sum additivity, combined with the classical \emph{casting out nines} property of decimal representation, we derive the modular signature of a vampire: the congruences $v \equiv x + y \pmod 9$ and $v \equiv x + y \pmod 3$. These in turn yield a \emph{fang taboo}---neither fang may be congruent to $1$ modulo $3$---and, modulo nine, confine the residue pair of the fangs to a small explicit solution set. We emphasize a deliberately acyclic logical architecture: the combinatorial additivity results are proved with no reference to congruences, and the congruences are then obtained as strict downstream consequences. We complement the theory with algorithms for enumerating each species of monster, a numerical study up to $10^8$, and a discussion of open density conjectures whose difficulty is tied to that of integer factorization.
\end{abstract}

\maketitle

\section{Introduction}

The number $1260$ satisfies $1260 = 21 \times 60$, and the multiset of digits of the two factors, $\{2,1\} \cup \{6,0\} = \{0,1,2,6\}$, coincides with the multiset of digits of $1260$ itself. Such numbers were popularized by Clifford Pickover under the evocative name \emph{vampire numbers}: the product is reconstituted from the digits of its two \emph{fangs}. Beyond their recreational appeal, vampire numbers pose genuine combinatorial questions about the interaction between the multiplicative structure of the integers and the additive/positional structure of their decimal representation.

This paper offers a clean foundational treatment. Our thesis is that the entire elementary theory is best organized around the \textbf{multiset of decimal digits}, denoted $M(n)$, and its two natural numerical projections: the \textbf{digit sum} $S(n)$, the total of the entries of $M(n)$; and the \textbf{digit length} $L(n)$, the number of entries of $M(n)$. The defining condition of a fang factorization becomes the single multiset equation $M(v) = M(x) + M(y)$ (with $+$ denoting multiset union), and every elementary property we prove is a corollary of manipulating this equation.

A methodological point guides the development. In the folklore, the digit-sum additivity of a fang factorization and the \emph{casting out nines} congruence are often intertwined, and it is easy to argue in a circle. We keep them strictly separated:
\begin{enumerate}[label=(\arabic*)]
  \item \textbf{Combinatorial layer.} Digit sum and digit length are the total and the cardinality of the digit multiset, and both are additive across a fang pair. These facts use \emph{only} the homomorphism properties of multiset sum and cardinality---no modular arithmetic.
  \item \textbf{Congruence layer.} We then invoke casting out nines, $n \equiv S(n) \pmod 9$ (and its mod-$3$ shadow), and combine it with digit-sum additivity to obtain the vampire's modular signature.
\end{enumerate}
The dependency is one-directional: congruences are consequences of the combinatorial layer, never hypotheses for it.

\section{Definitions}

Throughout, $n$ ranges over the nonnegative integers and all digits are base ten. We write $\dig(n)$ for the finite list of decimal digits of $n$ (least significant first); it is empty for $n = 0$.

\begin{definition}[digit multiset, digit sum, digit length]
For $n \in \mathbb{N}$, define
\[
M(n) := \text{the multiset of entries of } \dig(n), \quad
S(n) := \sum_{d \in \dig(n)} d, \quad
L(n) := |\dig(n)|,
\]
where the sum in $S(n)$ counts digits with multiplicity and $L(n)$ is the number of digits. Equivalently $S(n) = \msum M(n)$ and $L(n) = |M(n)|$.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[fang pair]
An ordered triple $(v, x, y)$ of positive integers is a \textbf{fang pair} (and $(x,y)$ are \emph{fangs} of $v$) if
\[
M(v) = M(x) + M(y) \qquad\text{and}\qquad x \cdot y = v,
\]
where $+$ denotes multiset union. We write $\Fang(v, x, y)$.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[vampire number]
A positive integer $v$ is a \textbf{vampire number} if $L(v) = 2n$ is even and it admits a fang pair $(v, x, y)$ with $L(x) = L(y) = n$ and $x, y$ not both divisible by $10$. A vampire with more than one essentially distinct fang pair is a \emph{multiple vampire}.
\end{definition}

\begin{proposition}[equivalence of formulations]
For all $v, x, y$,
\[
M(v) = M(x) + M(y) \iff \dig(v) \text{ is a permutation of } \dig(x) \mathbin{+\!\!+} \dig(y),
\]
where $\mathbin{+\!\!+}$ denotes list concatenation.
\end{proposition}

\begin{proof}
Two lists are permutations of one another iff they induce equal multisets, and concatenation of lists corresponds to union of induced multisets. Hence $M(v) = M(x)+M(y)$ is exactly the statement that $\dig(v)$ and $\dig(x)\mathbin{+\!\!+}\dig(y)$ induce the same multiset.
\end{proof}

\section{The Combinatorial Layer}

The two workhorse lemmas identify $S$ and $L$ as the total and cardinality of the digit multiset, and exploit that both are additive under multiset union. \emph{No modular arithmetic appears in this section.}

\begin{lemma}[digit sum is the multiset total]\label{lem:sumtotal}
For every $n$, $S(n) = \msum M(n)$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}
$M(n)$ is the multiset induced by $\dig(n)$, and the sum of a multiset induced by a list equals the sum of the list, namely $S(n)$.
\end{proof}

\begin{lemma}[digit length additivity]
If $\Fang(v, x, y)$ then $L(v) = L(x) + L(y)$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}
Cardinality $|\cdot|$ is a monoid homomorphism: $|A + B| = |A| + |B|$. Applying it to $M(v) = M(x)+M(y)$ and using $L(n)=|M(n)|$ gives $L(v) = L(x)+L(y)$.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[balanced fangs]
A vampire of length $2n$ has fangs of length exactly $n$ each.
\end{corollary}

\begin{lemma}[digit sum additivity]
If $\Fang(v, x, y)$ then $S(v) = S(x) + S(y)$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}
The total map $\msum(\cdot)$ is a monoid homomorphism: $\msum(A+B) = \msum A + \msum B$. By Lemma~\ref{lem:sumtotal}, $S(n) = \msum M(n)$. Applying $\msum$ to $M(v) = M(x)+M(y)$ gives $S(v) = S(x)+S(y)$.
\end{proof}

\section{The Congruence Layer}

\begin{lemma}[casting out nines / threes]\label{lem:castout}
For every $n$, $\ n \equiv S(n) \pmod 9\ $ and $\ n \equiv S(n) \pmod 3$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}
Write $n = \sum_i d_i 10^i$. Since $10 \equiv 1 \pmod 9$, each $10^i \equiv 1$, so $n \equiv \sum_i d_i = S(n) \pmod 9$. Reducing modulo $3$ (as $3 \mid 9$) gives the mod-$3$ statement.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[vampire law modulo 9]\label{thm:mod9}
If $\Fang(v, x, y)$ then $v \equiv x + y \pmod 9$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Chain: $v \equiv S(v) = S(x)+S(y) \equiv x + y \pmod 9$. The first congruence is casting out nines for $v$; the equality is digit-sum additivity; the last is casting out nines applied to $x$ and $y$ and added.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[vampire law modulo 3]\label{thm:mod3}
If $\Fang(v, x, y)$ then $v \equiv x + y \pmod 3$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Identical to Theorem~\ref{thm:mod9}, using the mod-$3$ half of Lemma~\ref{lem:castout}.
\end{proof}

\noindent\emph{Interpretation.} Although $v = x\cdot y$ multiplicatively, modulo $9$ (and $3$) the value $v$ agrees with the additive combination $x+y$: a vampire is simultaneously a product and, modulo nine, a sum of the same two fangs. This necessary constraint is available without performing the multiplication.

\section{Obstructions}

\begin{theorem}[fang taboo modulo 3]\label{thm:taboo}
If $\Fang(v, x, y)$ then $x \not\equiv 1 \pmod 3$ and $y \not\equiv 1 \pmod 3$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Since $v = xy$, Theorem~\ref{thm:mod3} gives $xy \equiv x + y \pmod 3$. If $x \equiv 1$, then $xy \equiv y$ and $x + y \equiv 1 + y$, whence $y \equiv 1 + y$, i.e. $0 \equiv 1 \pmod 3$, a contradiction. The case $y \equiv 1$ is symmetric.
\end{proof}

Equivalently, rearranging $xy \equiv x+y$ as $(x-1)(y-1) \equiv 1$: a factor $\equiv 1$ makes the left side $\equiv 0 \not\equiv 1$. For $1260 = 21\times 60$: $21 \equiv 0$ and $60 \equiv 0 \pmod 3$, both avoiding the forbidden residue $1$.

\begin{proposition}[mod-nine residue confinement]\label{prop:confine}
If $\Fang(v, x, y)$ then $(x - 1)(y - 1) \equiv 1 \pmod 9$. Hence $(x \bmod 9, y \bmod 9)$ lies in the solution set of $(a-1)(b-1)\equiv 1 \pmod 9$: since the units modulo $9$ are $\{1,2,4,5,7,8\}$, there are exactly $6$ admissible values of $a-1$, each determining $b-1 = (a-1)^{-1}$.
\end{proposition}

\begin{proof}
From $v = xy$ and Theorem~\ref{thm:mod9}, $xy \equiv x+y$, so $xy - x - y + 1 \equiv 1$, i.e. $(x-1)(y-1) \equiv 1 \pmod 9$. A product equal to the unit $1$ forces each factor to be a unit; the group $(\mathbb{Z}/9)^\times$ has order $6$, and $x-1$ determines $y-1$ uniquely.
\end{proof}

These two results reject the majority of candidate factorizations using only residues, before any digit comparison---the backbone of the pruning step in the enumeration below.

\section{The Extended Bestiary}

Let $D(n)$ denote the set of distinct digits of $n$ (the support of $M(n)$).

\begin{definition}[werewolf number]
$v$ is a \textbf{werewolf number} if $v = x\cdot y$ for nontrivial $x,y$ with $|D(v) \cap (D(x)\cup D(y))| = 1$ (the fangs share exactly one distinct digit with $v$).
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[ghost number]
$v$ is a \textbf{ghost number} if $v = x\cdot y$ for nontrivial $x,y$ with $D(v) \cap (D(x)\cup D(y)) = \varnothing$ (the fangs share no digit with $v$).
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[zombie / boundary number]
A \textbf{zombie number} is a product $v = x\cdot y$ satisfying $M(v) = M(x)+M(y)$ but violating a fine-print clause of the vampire definition (a prime fang, or the length-balance/trailing-zero conditions). E.g. factorizations of $125460$ mixing a prime and a composite.
\end{definition}

\begin{theorem}[ghosts have density zero, heuristic]\label{thm:ghost}
The proportion of ghost numbers among integers with $d$ digits tends to $0$ as $d \to \infty$; it is bounded above by $C\rho^{d}$ for constants $C>0$, $0<\rho<1$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
For $v = xy$ to be a ghost, every digit in $x$ or $y$ must be absent from $v$. Modeling the $d$ digits of $v$ as approximately uniform and independent, the probability a fixed digit value is absent from all positions is $(9/10)^d$. Requiring the whole set $D(x)\cup D(y)$ absent is at most $(9/10)^d$; a union bound over the boundedly many digit values and polynomially many factorizations gives $C\rho^d$ with $\rho = 9/10$.
\end{proof}

By contrast, vampires and werewolves become relatively more abundant as $d$ grows, since the number of realizable digit rearrangements increases combinatorially.

\section{Algorithms and Numerical Results}

The efficient route enumerates fang pairs. For a target length $2n$, iterate over $n$-digit pairs $(x,y)$ with $x \le y$, form $v = xy$, and accept when $L(v) = 2n$ and $M(v) = M(x)+M(y)$; the modular sieve of \S5 prunes before the multiset comparison.

\begin{verbatim}
Algorithm ENUMERATE-VAMPIRES(n):
  lo <- 10^(n-1); hi <- 10^n - 1
  for x from lo to hi:
    for y from x to hi:
      if x % 10 == 0 and y % 10 == 0: continue
      if (x-1)*(y-1) mod 9 != 1: continue     # residue confinement
      if x % 3 == 1 or y % 3 == 1: continue    # fang taboo
      v <- x*y
      if digit_length(v) != 2n: continue
      if digit_multiset(v) == digit_multiset(x)+digit_multiset(y):
        record (x,y) as fangs of v
\end{verbatim}

The double loop examines $O(10^{2n})$ pairs; the residue tests are $O(1)$ and remove a constant fraction, and the multiset check is $O(n)$. Enumeration reproduces the census: the smallest vampire is $1260 = 21\times 60$; the four-digit vampires are $1260, 1395, 1435, 1530, 1827, 2187, 6880$; and $125460 = 204\times 615 = 246\times 510$ is a double vampire. Every enumerated fang pair satisfies the modular signature and the taboo, and ghosts thin out rapidly with digit length, consistent with Theorem~\ref{thm:ghost}.

\section{Open Conjectures}

\begin{conjecture}[density decay]
The density of vampires in $[10^{2n}, 10^{2n+1}]$ tends to $1/\sqrt{n}$ as $n \to \infty$.
\end{conjecture}

\begin{conjecture}[never extinct]\label{conj:extinct}
Every even-length band $[10^{2k}, 10^{2k+2}]$ contains a vampire. Constructively: an explicit family of fang pairs, each a digit-balanced core plus a controlled tail, whose products land in the band while permuting their digits, giving vampires in every even-length block with bounded gaps.
\end{conjecture}

\begin{conjecture}[mod-nine equidistribution]
Over all vampires up to a growing bound, the fang residue pair modulo $9$ equidistributes across exactly the admissible classes of Proposition~\ref{prop:confine} and never strays outside them.
\end{conjecture}

\begin{conjecture}[stacked modular sieve]
Layering independent digit-sum-driven congruences across several moduli yields a single cheap test every vampire survives, yet whose non-vampire survivors retain density strictly between $0$ and $1$: powerful but provably incomplete.
\end{conjecture}

Deciding whether a given $v$ is a vampire, or counting vampires, requires searching over factorizations---tied to the hardness of integer factorization. The elementary constraints proved here are the part that escapes that hardness.

\section{Discussion and Future Work}

The fang condition is a single equation in the free commutative monoid of multisets over $\{0,\dots,9\}$, and the useful invariants---digit sum and length---are its two canonical homomorphisms, so additivity is automatic and the congruences are a thin classical layer. Keeping the two layers separate guarantees the congruences are genuine theorems. Digit-preserving conditions (vampires) proliferate; digit-avoiding ones (ghosts) suffer large-deviation extinction; partial ones (werewolves) interpolate. Future directions: arbitrary bases $b$ (casting out $b-1$); higher-arity factorizations $v = x_1\cdots x_k$; explicit constants in Theorem~\ref{thm:ghost}; and the constructive program of Conjecture~\ref{conj:extinct}.

